# THE TWO KINDS THAT BEAR DEMAND NOBODY SERVED. One row per holder filed as `customer` or
# `unrealised`.
#
# ⭐⭐ THIS RELATION IS USED THREE WAYS AND THAT IS WHY IT IS A FILE. As a SEMI-JOIN it says
#    "this layer admits somebody went unserved" -- which under a clearance fit is a
#    contradiction, since a supply above demand everywhere leaves nothing unmet. As an
#    ANTI-JOIN it says "this layer admits nobody", which under a measured-zero capacity and a
#    live exposure is a filing with a hole in it. Aggregated it is the quantity that bounds
#    the exposure. One relation, a semi-join, an anti-join and a sum.
#
# ⛔ THE OTHER THREE KINDS ARE NOT THE COMPLEMENT OF THIS ONE. `booked`, `counterparty` and
#   `people` all name somebody who ABSORBED the remainder; these two name demand that was
#   never met at all. That is a difference in kind, not in party.
-- pm:HolderKind values `customer` and `unrealised`.
SELECT h.*
FROM (
    :compose(entries/holders.sqlc)
) h
WHERE h.kind IN ('customer', 'unrealised')
